ADS-012 — Identifier Model

Document ID: ADS-012
Title: Identifier Model
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Canonical Identifier Model (CIM) for the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The Canonical Identifier Model establishes a standardized, globally unique, persistent, and technology-independent identification system for all Canonical Atlas Objects (CAOs), relationships, and governed entities.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Identifier architecture
Identifier types
Namespaces
Identifier lifecycle
Version identifiers
Reference model
Resolution model
Governance

This specification applies to every identifiable entity within Atlas.

3. Canonical Definition

A Canonical Identifier is a globally unique, immutable, and persistent identifier assigned to a Canonical Atlas Object or governed entity, enabling unambiguous reference, traceability, interoperability, and lifecycle continuity.

Identifiers establish identity.

They SHALL NOT encode business meaning.

4. Objectives

The Identifier Model SHALL:

Ensure global uniqueness.
Provide persistent identity.
Support distributed environments.
Enable stable references.
Support versioning.
Enable interoperability.
Preserve traceability.
Remain technology-independent.
5. Identifier Principles

Every Atlas identifier SHALL be:

Globally unique
Immutable
Persistent
Opaque
Stable
Resolvable
Version-aware
Independent of implementation

Identifiers SHALL NOT change because object content changes.

6. Identifier Categories

Atlas defines the following canonical identifier categories.

Identifier	Purpose
Object Identifier	Identifies a CAO
Relationship Identifier	Identifies a CRO
Version Identifier	Identifies a specific revision
Namespace Identifier	Identifies an organizational scope
Workflow Identifier	Identifies a workflow
Project Identifier	Identifies a project
Session Identifier	Identifies a communication session
Correlation Identifier	Links related activities
Transaction Identifier	Tracks transactional boundaries

Future categories SHALL extend this hierarchy.

7. Canonical Identifier Structure

Every identifier SHALL include the following conceptual components.

Component	Description
Namespace	Owning authority
Object Type	Canonical type classification
Identifier Value	Globally unique value
Version Reference	Optional version reference

These components define the logical structure of an identifier, independent of its physical representation.

8. Namespace Model

Namespaces SHALL:

Prevent identifier collisions.
Support organizational autonomy.
Enable federation.
Preserve uniqueness across deployments.

A namespace SHALL itself possess a unique identifier.

Example conceptual hierarchy:

Global Atlas Namespace
        │
        ├── Organization Namespace
        │       ├── Project Namespace
        │       └── Project Namespace
        │
        └── Organization Namespace

Namespaces SHALL NOT alter object identity.

9. Identifier Lifecycle

Identifiers SHALL progress through the following lifecycle.

Reserved
    │
Assigned
    │
Validated
    │
Active
    │
Referenced
    │
Archived

Once assigned, an identifier SHALL NEVER be reused.

10. Version Identification

Atlas distinguishes between:

Identity	Purpose
Object Identifier	Persistent identity across the object's lifetime
Version Identifier	Identity of a specific revision

Example:

Object ID
    │
    ├── Version 1
    ├── Version 2
    ├── Version 3
    └── Version 4

Version identifiers SHALL preserve lineage to the parent object identifier.

11. Reference Model

Atlas supports the following reference types.

Reference	Description
Direct Reference	References a specific object
Versioned Reference	References a specific version
Latest Reference	References the current approved version
Relative Reference	Context-dependent reference
External Reference	References entities outside Atlas

Reference semantics SHALL be explicit.

12. Resolution Model

Every identifier SHALL be resolvable.

Resolution SHALL determine:

Object existence
Canonical type
Current lifecycle state
Version information
Namespace
Governance metadata

Resolution mechanisms SHALL remain implementation-independent.

13. Traceability

Identifiers SHALL support traceability across:

Engineering artifacts
Decisions
Events
Workflows
Communications
Policies
Trust assertions
Relationships

Identifiers SHALL enable end-to-end navigation through the Atlas ecosystem.

14. Governance

Every identifier SHALL define:

Issuing authority
Namespace owner
Validation authority
Assignment policy
Retention policy
Resolution policy
Audit requirements

Identifier governance SHALL be independent of object content.

15. Quality Requirements

Every identifier SHALL satisfy:

Uniqueness
Persistence
Stability
Non-reusability
Resolvability
Traceability
Scalability
Interoperability

Quality SHALL be continuously verifiable.

16. Conformance

An Atlas implementation conforms to the Identifier Model if it:

Assigns globally unique identifiers.
Preserves identifier immutability.
Supports namespace management.
Supports version identifiers.
Enables identifier resolution.
Prevents identifier reuse.
Maintains identifier traceability.
17. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

Decentralized identifier (DID) compatibility.
Cross-enterprise namespace federation.
Persistent web identifiers.
Semantic identifier profiles.
Quantum-safe identifier generation.
Universal Atlas identifier registries.

Extensions SHALL preserve canonical identifier semantics.

